From 303cb8eea38c1caa1bfcf28f607dc4774a5d83ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Itsnatch <kamaumark996@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 11:08:33 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix: payday recoupment was login-triggered only and settled
 the wrong number
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Investigated a hunch that the "Outstanding Arrears" figure on the employer
Wallet page should get recouped to the admin's wallet on payday. It should —
but three separate gaps meant it never would have:

1. No automated trigger. The only thing that ever checked "is today this
   employer's payday" was a client-side effect that fired when an employer
   happened to load a dashboard page on that exact date. Miss that day and
   nothing catches up — there was no cron. Added
   app/api/cron/payday-recoupment (+ daily GitHub Actions workflow, same
   05:00 UTC pattern as the existing repayment-overdue check) that iterates
   every employer and creates a recoupment prompt for whoever's payday is
   today, notifying both the employer and admin.

2. Wrong number. The recoupment flow computed "amount due" by summing
   repayment_schedules rows — but employer_wallets.outstanding_liability
   (what the Wallet page actually displays as arrears, and what's genuinely
   owed under the deliberate loan-model design in payout-service.ts) is
   recorded in full at funding time, independent of repayment_schedules,
   which only exist for advances actually disbursed. An employer could
   carry real arrears with zero matching schedule rows, in which case the
   old flow would compute "0 due" and silently do nothing — structurally
   unable to ever collect it. amount_due now comes from
   outstanding_liability directly.

3. No way to settle liability not backed by a schedule row.
   repay_advance_to_admin requires a real advance_id. Added
   repay_employer_liability_to_admin (new RPC, no advance_id required) and
   wired applyPaydayRecoupmentCollection to settle real per-advance
   schedules first (keeps accurate advance-level repayment history), then
   apply any remainder directly against outstanding_liability.

Verified live end-to-end against the Notion Labs test employer (real
596,900 KES liability with zero backing repayment_schedules rows — exactly
the scenario this was blind to): triggered the new cron, confirmed a
payday_recoupments row was created for the correct amount, then settled it
and confirmed outstanding_liability went to 0 and the admin wallet gained
the exact USD-converted equivalent (98,445.55 -> 103,063.61).

Also includes two earlier migrations (max_advance_amount + advance_access_days
on employers/employer_onboarding) that were applied live during the EWA
Settings fix but not yet added to the tracked migrations folder.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
